1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,450
♪ (opening) ♪

2
00:00:05,450 --> 00:00:10,400
♪ ♪

3
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:15,559
Their tremendous skill is already proven —
the most talented girl group out there.

4
00:00:15,559 --> 00:00:20,680
They debuted on April 1, 2024, so it's been
about two years and eight months now,

5
00:00:20,680 --> 00:00:24,640
and this is already their sixth appearance
on Cultwo Show.

6
00:00:24,640 --> 00:00:26,720
Wow.

7
00:00:26,720 --> 00:00:33,000
Their YouTube channel has a whopping
13.5 million subscribers.

8
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:35,320
And the videos that passed
100 million views on YouTube—

9
00:00:35,320 --> 00:00:41,760
Really? There are nineteen of them in total.

10
00:00:42,600 --> 00:00:45,480
And as for videos that reached 200 million views—

11
00:00:45,480 --> 00:00:49,960
That's insane! Eleven in total.

12
00:00:49,960 --> 00:00:51,960
Oh my, that's crazy.

13
00:00:51,960 --> 00:00:57,559
On September 22 and 23, they sold out
every seat at the Kyocera Dome in Osaka, Japan,

14
00:00:57,559 --> 00:01:01,480
drawing 45,000 fans a day, 90,000 in total —

15
00:01:01,480 --> 00:01:07,439
the most beautiful babies in the world,
who had every one of them completely hooked.

16
00:01:08,040 --> 00:01:14,720
Now, the amazing girl group
behind all of these records —

17
00:01:14,720 --> 00:01:20,960
let's open with a live performance
by BABYMONSTER,

18
00:01:20,960 --> 00:01:24,240
a track from their 3rd mini album.
Give it up for "MOON"!

19
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:32,000
♪ "MOON" — live ♪

20
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:47,000
♪

21
00:01:53,840 --> 00:01:56,840
♪

22
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:11,000
♪

23
00:02:29,720 --> 00:02:34,659
♪

24
00:02:34,659 --> 00:02:39,599
♪ ♪

25
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:53,000
♪

26
00:03:06,239 --> 00:03:09,239
♪

27
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:23,000
♪

28
00:03:36,560 --> 00:03:43,319
♪ If you, if you, if you ♪

29
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:59,000
♪

30
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:13,500
♪

31
00:04:14,280 --> 00:04:20,479
Wow! That was BABYMONSTER's "MOON."

32
00:04:20,479 --> 00:04:21,840
Please take your seats.

33
00:04:21,840 --> 00:04:26,000
Ah, these dolls can really sing.

34
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:31,000
"Wow, their live is so good.
It sounds just like the studio version."

35
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:32,520
"It's even better than the recording."

36
00:04:32,520 --> 00:04:37,280
Right? Please have a seat in the order
shown on the screen here.

37
00:04:37,280 --> 00:04:38,759
Aren't they so pretty?

38
00:04:38,759 --> 00:04:39,639
Yes.

39
00:04:39,639 --> 00:04:46,280
This song is so hip. But MOON —
you know, I'm MOON myself.

40
00:04:46,280 --> 00:04:48,240
That's right, I'm the moon.

41
00:04:48,240 --> 00:04:49,360
I look just like a lump of moon.

42
00:04:49,360 --> 00:04:51,759
Oh — a full moon! Full moon.

43
00:04:51,759 --> 00:04:53,520
Yes. A full moon? A full moon.

44
00:04:53,520 --> 00:04:56,320
It's a full moon. A full moon.

45
00:04:56,320 --> 00:05:03,360
Now, our BABYMONSTER will say hello.
Shall we do the group greeting?

46
00:05:03,360 --> 00:05:05,800
Are you ready? — Yes, we're ready!

47
00:05:05,800 --> 00:05:11,240
Two, three! Hello!
We are BABYMONSTER!

48
00:05:16,199 --> 00:05:21,800
Now, having each of you simply
introduce yourselves would be no fun,

49
00:05:21,800 --> 00:05:28,280
so you know those ending poses
from music shows? We have a camera ready

50
00:05:28,280 --> 00:05:31,039
to catch them — introduce yourselves
while striking an ending pose.

51
00:05:31,039 --> 00:05:34,720
All right, shall we start with ASA?

52
00:05:34,720 --> 00:05:41,000
Here we go. Hello,
I'm ASA of BABYMONSTER.

53
00:05:41,199 --> 00:05:45,800
Oh, nice! A blown kiss.

54
00:05:45,800 --> 00:05:48,520
What should I do? — PHARITA, you're next.

55
00:05:48,520 --> 00:05:52,720
Hello, I'm PHARITA.

56
00:05:54,160 --> 00:05:56,120
Ah, that had a little aegyo mixed in.

57
00:05:56,120 --> 00:05:57,960
Now, RORA.

58
00:05:57,960 --> 00:06:01,199
I just got an order too. An order!

59
00:06:01,199 --> 00:06:04,120
Hello, I'm RORA of BABYMONSTER.

60
00:06:04,120 --> 00:06:07,120
Ah, lovely.

61
00:06:08,759 --> 00:06:13,120
Next up is CHIQUITA.

62
00:06:13,120 --> 00:06:16,759
Hello, I'm CHIQUITA.

63
00:06:17,400 --> 00:06:22,759
Now AHYEON? — I got an order too. Hello,

64
00:06:22,759 --> 00:06:26,280
I'm AHYEON of BABYMONSTER.

65
00:06:27,479 --> 00:06:29,440
So cute.

66
00:06:29,440 --> 00:06:32,280
And lastly, RUKA.

67
00:06:32,280 --> 00:06:38,520
Okay, nice. Hello,
I'm RUKA of BABYMONSTER.

68
00:06:38,520 --> 00:06:40,639
Love it.

69
00:06:40,639 --> 00:06:45,319
Over there, fans are holding up numbers
on sketchbooks — a four and a one.

70
00:06:45,319 --> 00:06:47,680
Ah, the fans are all putting in their orders.

71
00:06:47,680 --> 00:06:51,680
I honestly thought that person was an artist.
— Right?

72
00:06:51,680 --> 00:06:57,639
The song we just heard is a track from
their 3rd mini album, released this May.

73
00:06:57,639 --> 00:07:04,720
Our ASA, who loves seolleongtang, said that
of all the songs she's learned so far,

74
00:07:04,720 --> 00:07:11,000
she picked "MOON" as number one for the
hardest rap. — That's right.

75
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:12,840
Oh, really? Why is it so difficult?

76
00:07:12,840 --> 00:07:17,840
Is it the words? — No, the pronunciation.
It's a rap that leaves you out of breath.

77
00:07:17,840 --> 00:07:20,840
ASA, let's hear that difficult rap part.

78
00:07:20,840 --> 00:07:24,840
Right now? A cappella? — Yes, yes.

79
00:07:24,840 --> 00:07:31,560
You can do it, you can do it!
You can do it.

80
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:36,250
♪ (ASA performs the rap verse a cappella) ♪

81
00:07:36,250 --> 00:07:40,500
♪ ♪

82
00:07:40,919 --> 00:07:47,560
Wow — how much must you have practiced,
to glide through it in one breath like that?

83
00:07:47,560 --> 00:07:53,240
Like synchronized swimming —
you never come up for air. — Right.

84
00:07:53,240 --> 00:07:58,560
You're honestly so good. Now, before we
get into the talk, one announcement.

85
00:07:58,560 --> 00:08:04,639
We warmly welcome your sighting stories —
wherever you've spotted BABYMONSTER.

86
00:08:04,639 --> 00:08:10,080
And send in requests for the six members —
"Do this, do that" — by text message.

87
00:08:10,080 --> 00:08:13,777
Aegyo, rap, singing — anything goes.

88
00:08:13,777 --> 00:08:17,680
If you want something, text #1077 now.

89
00:08:17,680 --> 00:08:21,400
Short texts are 50 won,
and long texts are 100 won.

90
00:08:21,400 --> 00:08:25,919
While they were away from Cultwo Show,
we looked into how they've been.

91
00:08:25,919 --> 00:08:30,360
Oh, did you? — You're on a world tour
right now, aren't you? — "World to World."

92
00:08:30,360 --> 00:08:35,479
Wow. Eighteen cities, and a whopping...

93
00:08:35,479 --> 00:08:37,560
...31 shows in total.

94
00:08:37,560 --> 00:08:42,360
Wow. On their second world tour, they're
meeting overseas fans everywhere.

95
00:08:42,360 --> 00:08:46,040
But how did you release another album
in the middle of a world tour?

96
00:08:46,040 --> 00:08:48,320
We worked hard in between, in any spare moment.

97
00:08:48,320 --> 00:08:51,600
Oh my, oh my — did you record on the plane?

98
00:08:51,600 --> 00:08:54,920
Honestly, you all live such busy lives.

99
00:08:54,920 --> 00:09:00,839
And this Saturday and Sunday, November 7 and 8,
you're performing in Bangkok. — That's right.

100
00:09:00,839 --> 00:09:03,360
You're performing there? How wonderful.
PHARITA, CHIQUITA —

101
00:09:03,360 --> 00:09:04,240
Oh, that's right.

102
00:09:04,240 --> 00:09:10,959
That sounds so nice. How is it, PHARITA?
It must make you so happy.

103
00:09:10,959 --> 00:09:14,240
Going back to Thailand after so long —
friends come to see you, family comes too.

104
00:09:14,240 --> 00:09:15,200
Wow, how nice.

105
00:09:15,200 --> 00:09:17,040
CHIQUITA's family is coming too.
I'm so, so excited.

106
00:09:17,040 --> 00:09:21,640
They all come together — oh, a lot of them.
Ah, that sounds wonderful.

107
00:09:21,640 --> 00:09:27,519
The food you most want to eat in Thailand —
of all the local Thai dishes,

108
00:09:27,519 --> 00:09:32,040
if you had to recommend one to us Koreans?
PHARITA, one. CHIQUITA, one.

109
00:09:32,040 --> 00:09:35,040
Pu phat phong curry.

110
00:09:35,040 --> 00:09:37,600
Ah, pu curry. It's delicious, isn't it?

111
00:09:37,600 --> 00:09:40,959
When we went last time, RORA loved it too.
She really loved it.

112
00:09:40,959 --> 00:09:45,279
There are lots of Thai restaurants
in Korea now, though. Compared to eating it there,

113
00:09:45,279 --> 00:09:50,640
how does it compare? — Maybe it's that local,
eating-it-right-there feeling, but

114
00:09:50,640 --> 00:09:56,720
it tastes so much better there.
It felt softer, with a deeper umami.

115
00:09:56,720 --> 00:09:58,760
Oh — soft and savory.

116
00:09:58,760 --> 00:10:03,079
How do you pronounce it exactly?
— Pu phat phong curry.

117
00:10:03,079 --> 00:10:07,839
"Pu— phat— phong— curry."
Just like that. — And CHIQUITA?

118
00:10:07,839 --> 00:10:12,839
For me, it's moo kata.
— Moo kata.

119
00:10:12,839 --> 00:10:17,440
For those who know moo kata —
if it has "moo" in it, that's pork, right?

120
00:10:17,440 --> 00:10:19,360
That's right, pork. — Yes.

121
00:10:19,360 --> 00:10:23,079
Ah, and — she's such a foodie.
— A total foodie.

122
00:10:23,079 --> 00:10:24,399
It's a pork dish.

123
00:10:24,399 --> 00:10:31,600
It's like pork belly meets shabu-shabu —
a mix of the two. It's so delicious.

124
00:10:31,600 --> 00:10:34,200
I need to write that name down.
What was it again?

125
00:10:34,200 --> 00:10:37,680
"Moo kata." "Moo kata." "Moo-ka-ta."

126
00:10:37,680 --> 00:10:41,320
We're looking at a photo of it now —
that looks so delicious.

127
00:10:41,320 --> 00:10:45,279
Ah, that looks tasty.
It really does.

128
00:10:45,279 --> 00:10:46,839
Great reactions all around.

129
00:10:46,839 --> 00:10:52,160
And it's not just pork belly —
there's seafood in it too. — It's delicious.

130
00:10:52,160 --> 00:10:53,600
Wow — there's no way that wouldn't be good.

131
00:10:53,600 --> 00:10:57,880
It looks amazing. And that's not a red broth,
it's a white broth, right?

132
00:10:57,880 --> 00:11:00,800
Looks so good. Moo kata...

133
00:11:00,800 --> 00:11:08,040
Moo kata. Yes. Pu phat phong curry. Yes.
— So, there we have it.

134
00:11:08,040 --> 00:11:12,560
As I mentioned earlier, the Kyocera Dome
in Osaka seats around 45,000.

135
00:11:12,560 --> 00:11:16,600
I've actually been there myself.
I went to see GD's concert there.

136
00:11:16,600 --> 00:11:22,079
Wow — no, last time, for GD's solo tour,
my son was such a huge fan,

137
00:11:22,079 --> 00:11:25,240
and even though GD performed in Korea too,
he went to the Japan show — and the Korean one.

138
00:11:25,240 --> 00:11:26,120
Ah — wow.

139
00:11:26,120 --> 00:11:29,720
That's how into it he was. — The seats
are so spacious there, and the dome is huge.

140
00:11:29,720 --> 00:11:35,000
Right, 45,000 seats is really big.
And selling out both days —

141
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:38,920
that's 90,000 seats. An incredible record.
An incredible record.

142
00:11:38,920 --> 00:11:41,966
But here's the important part: it was a concert

143
00:11:41,966 --> 00:11:47,279
in Japan, and every member spoke to the audience
in Japanese, with no interpreter.

144
00:11:47,279 --> 00:11:53,680
Wait — wasn't it just ASA and RUKA?
— We speak it too, and the other members—

145
00:11:53,680 --> 00:11:55,120
— their Japanese is—

146
00:11:55,120 --> 00:11:59,740
(demonstrating

147
00:11:59,740 --> 00:12:03,040
Japanese)

148
00:12:03,040 --> 00:12:06,839
You're good! You learned well.

149
00:12:06,839 --> 00:12:12,720
I learned it from the members.
They teach me, and I've been learning a lot

150
00:12:12,720 --> 00:12:15,399
in one-on-one lessons with a teacher, too —
so I can communicate with our fans.

151
00:12:15,399 --> 00:12:18,839
Yes. — Wow. So then,

152
00:12:18,839 --> 00:12:23,440
how was it for you two Japanese members?
90,000 seats sold out, right there at home.

153
00:12:23,440 --> 00:12:28,079
Actually, we Japanese members
were the ones who fumbled more.

154
00:12:28,079 --> 00:12:34,079
Really? — I was nervous speaking Japanese,
and we had so many lines to say,

155
00:12:34,079 --> 00:12:41,160
so I got a bit mixed up and stumbled —
but the Korean members next to me helped.

156
00:12:41,160 --> 00:12:46,399
Oh. Your families all came, didn't they?
RUKA, your family too. How were they?

157
00:12:46,399 --> 00:12:51,360
It was amazing. I rode the trolley —
this time we rode the trolley for the encore,

158
00:12:51,360 --> 00:12:57,600
and I could see them right there.
My family was so moved. They cried.

159
00:12:57,600 --> 00:12:58,800
Wow.

160
00:12:58,800 --> 00:13:05,199
And she's the one who cries the least.
Almost never. — So she shed tears that day.

161
00:13:05,199 --> 00:13:08,760
When you see your family like that
while you're performing... — Yes.

162
00:13:08,760 --> 00:13:12,800
Could they be watching this broadcast
right now, in Japan? — They might be.

163
00:13:12,800 --> 00:13:16,839
Then say a word to your families.
We have about 40 seconds left.

164
00:13:16,839 --> 00:13:21,199
Say something in Japanese.

165
00:13:21,400 --> 00:13:25,000
(RUKA and ASA greet their families in Japanese)

166
00:13:25,240 --> 00:13:31,440
Nice. The important thing is, I understood.
Fighting! Yes — ganbarimasu.

167
00:13:31,440 --> 00:13:35,645
In Part 2, we'll hear one more live song

168
00:13:35,645 --> 00:13:39,639
together and chat about this and that.

169
00:13:39,639 --> 00:13:44,720
We'll be talking about the newly released
"You Deserve It." Just wait a little.

170
00:13:44,720 --> 00:13:49,240
Send in lots of texts. Part 2 with
BABYMONSTER is coming up.

171
00:13:50,600 --> 00:13:54,933
Countless idols are promoting in Korea and

172
00:13:54,933 --> 00:13:58,440
touring the world spreading K-pop,

173
00:13:58,440 --> 00:14:05,480
but there's no loyalty like this — coming to
Cultwo Show six times, with every release.

174
00:14:05,480 --> 00:14:11,199
Thank you so much. — We should thank you.
It's our sixth appearance — basically the most ever.

175
00:14:11,199 --> 00:14:15,759
I think it nearly is. Among girl groups —
yeah. It's an honor.

176
00:14:15,759 --> 00:14:20,480
Whenever a new song comes out, they come, no question.
There's one boy group just like that.

177
00:14:20,480 --> 00:14:23,880
BOYNEXTDOOR. BOYNEXTDOOR is like that too.

178
00:14:23,880 --> 00:14:27,040
Still, the two of you are about neck and neck now.

179
00:14:27,040 --> 00:14:30,480
Thank you so much. We're here with BABYMONSTER,
the girl group of loyalty, and the other day

180
00:14:30,480 --> 00:14:34,913
on Cultwo Show, you mentioned that Korean words

181
00:14:34,913 --> 00:14:38,120
are trending among Gen Z in Japan.

182
00:14:38,120 --> 00:14:42,639
Oh, I've seen an article about that.
RUKA, ASA — do you really know about this?

183
00:14:42,639 --> 00:14:47,120
We actually use it a lot ourselves.
— Oh? How do you use it?

184
00:14:47,120 --> 00:14:52,240
Like, um, what would be an example...

185
00:14:52,240 --> 00:14:57,920
"Jinjja" just slips out — that kind of thing.
— Yes, that feeling.

186
00:14:57,920 --> 00:15:02,959
Oh, that's how it's used? — Yes.
It mixes in and pops out suddenly.

187
00:15:02,959 --> 00:15:08,440
"Ah, gidaryeo-kudasai!" — "Ah, wait."
"Gidaryeo. Kudasai." — You really say it like that?

188
00:15:08,440 --> 00:15:12,000
Uh-huh.

189
00:15:14,199 --> 00:15:16,966
It comes out

190
00:15:16,966 --> 00:15:22,269
naturally, like this —

191
00:15:22,269 --> 00:15:26,419
naturally, dropped

192
00:15:26,419 --> 00:15:30,800
in here and there.

193
00:15:30,800 --> 00:15:36,440
"Ah, jinjja kawaii!" — like that.
— You really do talk like that.

194
00:15:36,440 --> 00:15:41,959
My friends really love it. They love
the word "jinjja." — Oh, they love saying "jinjja."

195
00:15:41,959 --> 00:15:45,519
Ah — "Jinjja cute, really."

196
00:15:45,519 --> 00:15:51,680
Back in our day, we used Japanese words
like "ippai" all the time, too. It's like mixing in

197
00:15:51,680 --> 00:15:54,199
English: "Ippai — it's delicious," like that.

198
00:15:54,199 --> 00:16:01,519
Right, right, right. Like dropping in "nice."
— Yes, you just use it like that.

199
00:16:01,519 --> 00:16:08,160
I feel quite proud, as a Korean,
now that Korean is spreading everywhere.

200
00:16:08,160 --> 00:16:11,560
Now, a BABYMONSTER sighting story has arrived.

201
00:16:11,560 --> 00:16:15,040
Who will read it? Shall RORA read one?
— Yes.

202
00:16:15,040 --> 00:16:17,497
From 7404: "I work at the airport, so I see

203
00:16:17,497 --> 00:16:21,839
BABYMONSTER often — and PHARITA is truly
the prettiest person in the world."

204
00:16:21,839 --> 00:16:27,199
"The prettiest in the world," they say.
— Oh. Thank you so much.

205
00:16:27,199 --> 00:16:30,399
Oh, the person who saw her is right here?
Where are you sitting?

206
00:16:30,399 --> 00:16:33,600
I'm a fan of PHARITA unnie.

207
00:16:33,600 --> 00:16:39,720
So you came here today? You skipped out
on the airport to come? — Yes. Yes.

208
00:16:39,720 --> 00:16:44,279
Then the planes won't be taking off.
With you gone. — Yes.

209
00:16:44,279 --> 00:16:48,639
Now, our PHARITA, the prettiest in the world,
please draw a gift for them. Right here.

210
00:16:48,639 --> 00:16:52,592
A gift? — Since they're here in person,

211
00:16:52,592 --> 00:16:56,240
PHARITA, draw one for them yourself.

212
00:16:56,240 --> 00:17:03,519
What will it be? Draw one and
announce it yourself. Here —

213
00:17:03,519 --> 00:17:09,280
A cucumber? — What could it be?
"1 kg of ice."

214
00:17:09,280 --> 00:17:14,959
"1 kg of ice." — "1 kg of ice."

215
00:17:14,959 --> 00:17:20,480
In winter, of all seasons — 1 kg of ice.

216
00:17:20,480 --> 00:17:25,360
Oh, nice. — Yes. Ice... I'll freeze to death.
Yes, yes, yes.

217
00:17:25,360 --> 00:17:27,568
You don't have to clap into the mic, it's fine —

218
00:17:27,568 --> 00:17:30,559
the thumping gets picked up.
Now, 7199's text — who will read it?

219
00:17:30,559 --> 00:17:33,039
Shall we have ASA read this one?

220
00:17:33,039 --> 00:17:40,520
Yes. From 7199: "This August, I saw you all
at the airport as you were departing.

221
00:17:40,520 --> 00:17:46,640
A huge crowd had gathered — it was BABYMONSTER.
Especially AHYEON's abs — a total show-stealer."

222
00:17:46,640 --> 00:17:50,160
Wait, why would you see abs at an airport?

223
00:17:50,160 --> 00:17:57,559
Ah, that's the airport fashion, then.
August — because it was summer.

224
00:17:57,559 --> 00:18:00,360
Yes. They're curious what exercise you enjoy.

225
00:18:00,360 --> 00:18:05,600
What kind of exercise do you do
to get abs like that?

226
00:18:05,600 --> 00:18:07,400
Did you run to the airport? — Yes.

227
00:18:07,400 --> 00:18:11,559
Yes. Oh, that's so cool. It really feels
like you ran here. — Right? Yes.

228
00:18:11,559 --> 00:18:15,555
Honestly, I don't work out as intensely

229
00:18:15,555 --> 00:18:19,960
